      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) in a rectangular prism, we can use the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. The diagonal \\( d \\) of a rectangular prism with side lengths \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2} \\]\n\nFor this rectangular prism, the side lengths are \\( a = 10 \\) inches, \\( b = 20 \\) inches, and \\( c = 10 \\) inches. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2} \\]\n\nCalculating the squares of the side lengths, we have:\n\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 20^2 = 400 \\]\n\\[ 10^2 = 100 \\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[ 100 + 400 + 100 = 600 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of 600, we get:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{100} \\times \\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}} \\]",
